00:00:03.704 --> 00:00:05.370
MATT BLUMBERG: My
name is Matt Blumberg.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.140
I'm a Product Manager
on the Chrome Team.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.139
And today I'm going to
talk about Chrome browser

00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:10.480
in the enterprise.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.510
Leave some time
at the end for Q&amp;A

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.470
and also note that we have a
space, the Android and Chrome

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.730
sandbox and Mosconi
on the third floor.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:23.438
So please stop by there
tomorrow if you have time.

00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:26.200
So this is just a quick
overview of the agenda.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.150
Again really just going to
cover a lot of the features

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.420
for Chrome in the enterprise.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:36.180
And again happy to take
questions at the end.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.307
So first we're going
to cover why would

00:00:38.307 --> 00:00:39.890
your company want
to switch to Chrome?

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.150
What are some of the
benefits of using

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.320
Chrome in your enterprise?

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.680
By show of hands who's using
Chrome today at the office?

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.270
OK, so mission accomplished.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.820
So starting off with
our key pillars,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.730
every feature that we design
has these key pillars in mind.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.890
So when we start with speed we
know speed, is very important,

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.810
and it's every second
counts when you're

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.470
trying to complete a task.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.920
And we want Chrome to be as
responsive and conservative

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.102
with system resources
as possible.

00:01:11.102 --> 00:01:13.560
From a simplicity perspective,
we want to delight our users

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.440
and optimize for predictability
with concise actions

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.380
and solutions.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.630
And we know security is one
of the most critical areas

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:21.980
for enterprises.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.950
And we want to be as transparent
regarding security and privacy

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.340
by ensuring users are informed
in non-technical terms.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.280
I'm sure you've seen Aw Snap
or green padlocks, red lines

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.230
if the certificates aren't
valid on the Omnibox.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.092
And we safeguard
our users by letting

00:01:39.092 --> 00:01:41.300
them know that they're
protected as well as warn them

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.550
before reaching malicious sites.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.310
And from a manageability
perspective,

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.320
we want to give
IT Administrators

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.110
every bit of control over their
end users experience possible.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.625
So in the old days, you'd have
a Native App for opening PDFs

00:01:57.625 --> 00:01:59.500
and another one for
spreadsheets, another one

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.796
for documents and so on.

00:02:01.796 --> 00:02:03.670
But we really see the
web browser as a window

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:06.640
into all of your enterprise
applications in the future.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.979
Software upgrades can be
deployed with a simple refresh

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:10.770
of the web page.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.110
Chrome offers a better
experience for users,

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.320
complete manageability for IT
and the latest web technology

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.880
for developers to
build on top of.

00:02:20.671 --> 00:02:22.420
So I'm sure many of
you checked your phone

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.890
in the morning, maybe
one on your laptop

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.180
and got some things done before
coming to the conference.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.400
Maybe throughout a normal
day in between meetings

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.920
you go to your desk and
have a desktop PC there.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.830
In the evening maybe you check
some emails on a computer

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:39.100
at home or on a tablet.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.934
The browser becomes
the universal platform

00:02:40.934 --> 00:02:42.100
across all of these devices.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.740
And what they have in common
is that they're all connected.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.370
The platforms may differ but
why shouldn't your experience

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.280
be seamless across
every device you use.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.710
By signing into Chrome
services, like Chrome Sync

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.612
will allow users to
easily and quickly pick

00:03:00.612 --> 00:03:01.820
up right where they left off.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.640
So today every application
must run on every device.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.680
Your users expect
that, and they expect

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.560
it to be seamless across
corporate issued and often

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.050
times personal devices.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.940
The fact is that today's
information workers,

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.490
on average use 10 web
apps across three devices

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.740
to do his or her job.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.540
Today's best business
applications,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.930
like Salesforce and G Suite,
Work Day and many others

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.340
are all web based.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.910
Even traditional enterprise
vendors, like SAP,

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.580
are now building
their new applications

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:39.290
for the web based on HTML 5 and
they work very well in Chrome.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:41.997
To empower your employees with
today's best business tools

00:03:41.997 --> 00:03:43.580
companies have to
use the browser that

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.350
is the best support,
with the latest web

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.260
standards like HTML 5.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.630
We're also seeing a big uptake
in enterprise development

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.100
of progressive web apps.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.130
You may have heard more
about this in other sessions

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:56.180
at the conference.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.830
But all of the
features that you have

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.020
with Native Apps such
as push notifications,

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.600
offline functionality,
adding icons

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.490
to home screens
on mobile devices,

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:07.640
launching in full-screen
mode, access to the clipboard,

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.247
all of these things are possible
and progressive web apps,

00:04:10.247 --> 00:04:12.455
and they can run across any
device mobile or desktop.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.620
So when we launched Chrome
in September of 2008,

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.882
the world said, who needs
another web browser.

00:04:20.882 --> 00:04:22.340
And if we jump
forward to today, we

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.310
have more than 2 billion
users across Chrome desktop

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.690
and mobile.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:27.990
We are very close--

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.080
I believe today or tomorrow
launching Chrome 57.

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:35.150
And we are seeing HTML 5, and
progressive web app adoption

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.130
continue to gain traction
in the enterprise.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.680
Specifically, in the
past couple of years,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.660
Chrome has seen hyper growth
in the enterprise space.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:47.500
We are making large investments
on our enterprise engineering

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.350
resources as well as working
closely with our largest

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.500
enterprise partners who
are building SAS solutions

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.570
and aligning priorities
to ensure those solutions

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:56.770
work very well.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.320
And we're building the right
features for the future

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.850
for enterprise customers.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:05.050
It's just an overview here
of security and privacy.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:08.400
So we obviously take
security very seriously.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.720
And technologies like
Safe Browsing, Sandboxing

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.610
and our ability to
quickly deploy updates

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.860
are what makes Chrome the
most secure browser available.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:19.570
Activant, which is
now known as Octave

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:22.090
wrote an extensive report
comparing Chrome browser

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.360
security to other browsers in
the industry several years ago.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.812
And it goes into a lot of depth
on what the differentiators are

00:05:29.812 --> 00:05:31.270
from a Chrome
security perspective.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:33.799
So feel free to
review that report.

00:05:33.799 --> 00:05:35.590
We also have updated
content on our website

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:37.452
regarding Chrome
security features.

00:05:37.452 --> 00:05:39.910
And we will be refreshing the
security report in the coming

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.993
months so that you can
watch out for that as well.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.540
So as I mentioned, security
is one of our key pillars.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.822
And Chrome was designed
with security built in.

00:05:49.822 --> 00:05:52.030
And it's always top of mind
during all of our feature

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.140
development processes.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.065
So just to highlight a
few of these features.

00:05:56.065 --> 00:05:58.240
Chrome now offers the
ability to generate

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.190
safe and easy to use passwords.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:02.544
So these are passwords that
you don't have to remember,

00:06:02.544 --> 00:06:03.960
and you don't
really need to know,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.520
but they're accessible
at any time.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.800
Chrome visually reassures
users that we're

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.987
protecting them at the Omnibox
at the top of the screen.

00:06:11.987 --> 00:06:14.320
And as I'll talk about in a
minute more on safe browsing

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.084
we'll warn users when they
visit malicious sites.

00:06:18.084 --> 00:06:20.000
We do research to make
sure these warnings are

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.600
easy to understand and not scary
and complicated to end users.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.550
If Chrome detects that
a user's settings have

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:28.340
been tampered with by malware,
we'll reset those settings

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.232
automatically.

00:06:29.232 --> 00:06:30.190
No tickets to helpdesk.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:33.140
No other trouble
ticket is needed.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:36.710
So you always have full control
over the browser experience.

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:39.590
We also offer new features such
as ephemeral profiles, which

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:42.950
allows users to have the full
Chrome signed and experience,

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:45.620
extensions, customizations,
all of those things

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:48.980
can be used as a normal
user, but that profile

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:51.710
can automatically be wiped
when they close out Chrome.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.120
So we're seeing
a lot of interest

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.310
in this new environment
such as retail,

00:06:55.310 --> 00:07:00.070
hotels where their shared PCs
were that's a common scenario.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:02.650
You can also set a passphrase
for syncing your data

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.800
with Chrome to offer end to
end encryption of that data.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.320
Chrome's PDF reader
also leads industry

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.330
in the protection of
untrustworthy PDFs.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.330
So it's very, very
secure way to view PDFs.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.310
So earlier I mentioned
how Chrome updates are

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.680
a critical piece of our
story and the browser

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.840
has to be updated to be secure.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.860
So here's what we're showing---
so in this example here

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:31.410
we're showing the comparison
of speed updates from Safari

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:32.460
and Chrome.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.845
So as you can see
here, this data

00:07:34.845 --> 00:07:37.800
is from clicky.com, which
is a web analytics company.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.710
The shape of the
graph can show over

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:42.690
a period of months
for old versions

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.250
to get updated and die off.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.070
And you can see
Safari 8 from 2014

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.771
actually is still kicking along.

00:07:48.771 --> 00:07:51.270
So this is very consistent with
browsers with annual release

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.480
cycles and other browsers that
don't have automatic update

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.810
features.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:57.420
And when you compare
this to Chrome,

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.290
you can see the
beauty of auto update.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:01.920
Old versions disappear
very quickly.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:05.100
And so does access to those
known vulnerabilities.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.380
We have major releases
every six weeks,

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:09.030
and security updates
can be deployed

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.090
as needed whenever
there's a security

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:14.350
vulnerability discovered.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.180
By allowing Chrome
to auto update,

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.120
you won't need to wait until
Tuesday for security updates

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:20.700
to be deployed.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:23.446
Google can deploy those
updates at Google scale.

00:08:23.446 --> 00:08:25.320
So we can reach hundreds
of millions of users

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:28.182
around the world in
a matter of hours.

00:08:28.182 --> 00:08:30.140
So another security
feature I want to highlight

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:32.000
is Safe Browsing.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.100
Safe Browsing will show
users a warning message

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:37.950
before they visit a dangerous
site or download a harmful app.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.450
Our scanning infrastructure also
protects the Chrome Web Store

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.010
from potentially
harmful extensions.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.960
This is the same technology
that we use in the Play Store.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:48.460
Our technology
protects your company

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:51.700
from a range of malware,
phishing and social engineering

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:55.060
attacks that might try to steal
passwords or infect machines.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:56.980
If an employee encounters
websites suspected

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.440
of being deceptive or dangerous
while browsing the web,

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.217
they'll be shown
a warning message.

00:09:01.217 --> 00:09:03.550
We'll also show warning
messages on dangerous downloads,

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:06.400
like malware that could
inject ads or again jeopardize

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.850
your corporate network.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:13.590
How many of you have seen
this warning message at home?

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:14.840
OK, just a few.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:17.240
So OK good, so you're not
visiting sites with malware.

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:21.970
So discovering these
vulnerabilities

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:23.230
are very important to Google.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:24.646
And we've implemented
technologies

00:09:24.646 --> 00:09:26.800
such as fuzzing and
cluster fuzz, which

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:29.410
run thousands of virtual
instances of Chrome

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.650
running hundreds of millions
of automated tests every day.

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:35.110
We use this data to analyze
crashes and identify

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:36.550
regressions quickly.

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:40.120
Long before any of these
changes reach the stable channel

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:41.200
of Chrome.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.030
And we know we
can't do this alone.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:46.330
So we created the Chrome Reward
Program in January of 2010

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:48.940
to help reward the security
researchers and individuals who

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:51.700
invest their time and
energy into helping

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:53.530
us make Chrome more safe.

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:55.277
So we've paid out
more than $9 million

00:09:55.277 --> 00:09:57.610
since this program existed
and more than a third of that

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:58.568
was paid out last year.

00:09:58.568 --> 00:10:02.080
So more folks have been getting
involved in the near term.

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:06.860
I'm going to mention
a new feature

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.070
that we're working on related
to security plan for later

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:10.965
this year.

00:10:10.965 --> 00:10:12.590
So we're going to be
adding, even more,

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:14.960
controls for Enterprise Admins.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:17.390
Things such as location,
determine security level

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:19.520
of the client, as well
as user restrictions

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.160
will allow you to both expand
access to your corporate web

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.472
applications and at the
same time offer the controls

00:10:25.472 --> 00:10:27.430
you need to ensure the
experience is secure end

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:29.530
to end.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:32.560
So, for example, Chrome
support of security keys

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.270
from an administrator
perspective

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:36.706
can specify that their
employees and users

00:10:36.706 --> 00:10:38.080
have to be strongly
authenticated

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.030
to access a web application.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:41.980
From a device
management perspective,

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:43.870
you'll be able to
monitor client-side state

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:47.285
and configuration on
across all major platforms.

00:10:47.285 --> 00:10:48.910
So this enables the
state of the device

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.962
to be monitored by
the infrastructure

00:10:50.962 --> 00:10:52.420
so that the state
can be considered

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:54.994
when making an access decision.

00:10:54.994 --> 00:10:56.660
The management
capability will integrate

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.880
with existing device
management systems you already

00:10:58.880 --> 00:10:59.900
have running.

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.930
And including system checks
such as if the client has

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:05.651
current anti-virus software
or if the disk is encrypted.

00:11:05.651 --> 00:11:07.400
Things along those
lines will be reported,

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:10.190
and a decision will be
made as to allowing access.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:13.460
These are some of the
examples of the concerns

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.570
from our customers.

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:19.600
Talk a little bit about
speed and power improvements.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.970
So Chrome was built to
be fast in every way.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:25.450
We monitor every aspect of speed
regarding startup time, page

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:28.690
load time, video
rendering of graphics,

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:33.452
complex scripts, Javascript's
and business web applications.

00:11:33.452 --> 00:11:35.410
And we also work very
closely with our partners

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.150
to maintain top
performance with each

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.280
of their software releases.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:41.700
And of course, as you can
see with the Octane scores

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.450
this is a benchmark
of Javascript's speed.

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.180
There's been
significant improvements

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:47.320
in over the last
couple of years.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:54.560
So Chrome is stable because it
was built using multiprocessing

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.830
architecture which allocates
each task and every tab

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:00.750
running as a separate process.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.290
So while we constantly update
Chrome with new improvements,

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:05.120
we can quickly
solve any problems

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:06.916
that might cause the
browser to crash.

00:12:06.916 --> 00:12:09.290
So I'm sure if you've been
using Chrome for a long period

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:12.110
of time you may have seen a tab
crash but the entire instance

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:13.310
won't.

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:17.390
So again from a security
perspective processes are

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.920
isolated, and they cannot talk
across tabs with each other.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.210
So regarding power, over
the past several releases

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:29.420
we've made large improvements
to power consumption on Chrome,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:32.840
and we now have a large team
dedicated to power reduction.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:35.450
So this is just a small
example of the change

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:37.760
we made on mobile
devices where we can now

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:40.820
refresh only a small
part of a tile as opposed

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.110
to the whole screen.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:44.300
So this may seem like
a very minor change,

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:47.120
but this is the type
of ongoing effort

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:48.999
to reduce power and
energy consumption

00:12:48.999 --> 00:12:50.165
for battery powered devices.

00:12:53.347 --> 00:12:55.430
So I mentioned the tile
redraw, but there are also

00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:57.140
countless other
efforts across dozens

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:00.549
of components to reduce power
consumption across the board.

00:13:00.549 --> 00:13:02.090
So this is showing
the progress we're

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:05.267
making towards reducing
power usage in Chrome.

00:13:05.267 --> 00:13:07.850
And with each release, there has
been significant improvements

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:08.683
as you can see here.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.540
So I'll give a quick
update on media as well.

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.640
We've also made
large improvements

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:19.510
on bandwidth usage.

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:22.820
So we know that companies
use media in many ways.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:25.120
Everything ranging from
videoconferencing to now

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.340
news and media and social
media which are now

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.460
increasingly video based.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:33.370
Everything even internal
communications such as training

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:36.010
videos, company updates
are now shifting

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:39.070
to be streaming video to
enable access for employees

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:42.040
across multiple locations.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:44.440
Chrome has extensive
support for WebRTC which

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:47.860
is used for video conferencing.

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:50.366
And we are now working hard
to improve video codecs

00:13:50.366 --> 00:13:52.240
to decrease bandwidth
usage while maintaining

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.850
the same video quality.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:57.896
While not allowed to share
the exact YouTube stats,

00:13:57.896 --> 00:14:00.520
to give you a sense of the scale
of video watch time and Chrome

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.410
it's truly staggering.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:04.720
There are thousands
of years of video

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:05.910
played in Chrome every day.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:13.440
So what else can we do
to improve this area?

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.530
Chrome supports the latest
video codecs including VP9

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:18.616
to improve quality
and reduce bandwidth.

00:14:18.616 --> 00:14:20.490
So as you can see here,
the image on the left

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:22.770
is clear and uses
30% less bandwidth

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:25.140
than H264 which is on the right.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.480
Chrome also supports
DRM which allows

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:30.640
you to protect your
sensitive corporate videos.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.280
The support is deployed through
a component called Widevine

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.260
which is available
across Mac, Windows,

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:38.320
Linux Android and Chrome OS.

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:40.900
We also support--
we now allow users

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:44.980
to download secure content
and offline video playback

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:47.110
is supported as well.

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:48.820
When you playback
videos, Chrome can also

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:50.950
cast that to larger
screens or other devices

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:53.640
for easier viewing.

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.040
So as I mentioned, VP9
will say more than 30%

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:58.520
of video bandwidth
at the same quality.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:04.450
This can result in some 30%
savings on a video encoded

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:07.350
at a one megabit bit
rate for 1,000 employees

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:10.470
would equate to more than a gig
of mobile data savings per day.

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.630
And obviously, one megabit
video they rate is very low.

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:15.780
So it can obviously add up.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:18.450
In addition to video,
Chrome has support for WebP

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:21.090
which is a higher image quality
than JPEG of the same file

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:22.020
size.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:24.510
So for companies with large
internal image databases,

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.885
this also can improve visual
quality and save on storage.

00:15:30.004 --> 00:15:32.420
So I'm going to talk a little
bit about some of the Chrome

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:35.329
enterprise management features.

00:15:35.329 --> 00:15:37.620
So today there are a couple
hundred management features

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:40.572
that allow you to control the
Chrome experience end to end.

00:15:40.572 --> 00:15:42.030
Everything from
setting a home page

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:45.102
to force deploying extensions
to configuring and proxy servers

00:15:45.102 --> 00:15:46.560
all of these things
can be deployed

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.080
within your existing Active
Directory infrastructure

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:52.585
or through our
cloud-based console.

00:15:52.585 --> 00:15:54.960
It's the same Chrome browser
that uses are familiar with,

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.410
but now IT can customize it
to meet your company's needs.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.239
We are also now offering a 24/7
enterprise support offering

00:16:01.239 --> 00:16:03.030
should you need to
troubleshoot or escalate

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:06.210
any issues with our team.

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:08.640
Now we know very well that
moving to the open web

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:10.330
is not going to
happen overnight.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.990
And those legacy applications
from 10 and 15 years ago that

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.480
used Silverlight and Active-X
are not going anywhere

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.354
in the near future.

00:16:17.354 --> 00:16:19.270
We know that you rely
on those apps every day.

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:21.090
And we want to make it
as easy and seamless as

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.173
possible through to for
your users to access those

00:16:23.173 --> 00:16:24.880
applications.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:28.080
So we've built a tool called
Legacy Browser Support.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.750
And this will allow Admins to
configure Chrome as the default

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:34.350
browser and define a number of
sites it should still be opened

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.030
with an alternative browser.

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:39.930
When the user is done with the
Legacy browser and the Legacy

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:44.230
property, it will be seamlessly
switched back to Chrome

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:45.480
to resume where they left off.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.830
No training or change
management needed.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:52.040
So while Chrome's auto update
feature enables your company

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.560
to be on the safest
fastest version of Chrome,

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:56.630
we know that many companies
also want flexibility

00:16:56.630 --> 00:16:59.660
when making these types
of changes and upgrades.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.640
So you can choose to
leave auto update on

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:04.790
to get those security fixes.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:06.410
Or you can also
disable auto updates

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:08.690
and manage the timing of
updates on your own schedule

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:10.901
based on your
organization's needs.

00:17:10.901 --> 00:17:12.859
For example, you may only
want to allow updates

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:15.170
at certain times
or weekends, or you

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:16.670
want to deploy after
you've had more

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:18.146
time to test the new release.

00:17:18.146 --> 00:17:19.520
And those are all
fully supported

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:21.950
through group policy
that can be configured

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:23.060
across all platforms.

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:28.910
So Chrome connects to Google
for tools that require access

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:30.080
to our cloud.

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:32.870
For example, predictive
search or translate features

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.720
will need to connect to Google.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:39.110
You will always have control
to turn off these features

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.450
and control what data
is shared with Google.

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:43.025
Again all configurable
by policy.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:49.280
So as you run more and more
of your business on the web,

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:52.100
you can use policies to define
what absence extensions get

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:53.990
published to your employees.

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:55.532
In an upcoming
release of Chrome,

00:17:55.532 --> 00:17:57.740
we're going to be offering
Admins, even more, control

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:00.920
over applications and extensions
by allowing them to restrict

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.890
which extensions can be
deployed based on the permission

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:06.410
the app or extension declarers.

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:09.560
For example, today it's very
common for administrators

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:13.520
to control applications
through a list or block list.

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:15.650
With this new feature
instead of having

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:17.690
to kind of curate what
applications are allowed

00:18:17.690 --> 00:18:22.500
or blacklist applications that
you discover after the fact,

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:24.890
you'll be able to
restrict every extension,

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:26.330
for example, that
needs to access

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:28.040
the webcam or the microphone.

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:30.710
And then not have to
get into reviewing

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:32.960
each extension manually.

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:35.360
This also will include future
updates for extensions.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:38.840
So if you have a note taking
extension deployed today

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:41.690
and in the future it for some
reason needs a microphone,

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:44.240
that would automatically
be disabled

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:47.120
as you would have to monitor
every single update of Chrome

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:48.205
extensions.

00:18:51.254 --> 00:18:52.670
So Chrome is the
only browser that

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:54.230
is cross-platform
and can be managed

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:56.360
from a single
cloud-based console.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.277
And we believe the browser
should be stateless

00:18:58.277 --> 00:19:00.318
and that management should
be easy no matter what

00:19:00.318 --> 00:19:01.390
device the user picks up.

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:08.359
So we offer Chrome enterprise
support due to customer demand.

00:19:08.359 --> 00:19:10.650
So we understand that it's
a blocker for many companies

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:13.440
to move to Chrome if they're
not able to get enterprise level

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:16.530
support directly from Google.

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:18.330
So we began offering
this last year.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:21.870
And it's worth noting that
this is targeted specifically

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:23.220
for non-G Suite customers.

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:25.170
If you already are
a G Suite customer

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:28.230
this services included.

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:31.230
And this is more targeted for
larger enterprises typically

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:34.140
more than 1,000 users or more.

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:35.970
So that concludes my remarks.

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:36.780
Thank you.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:38.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

